 \textbf{Different goals.} CrackSQL and RISE target query migration: they translate one self-contained query or procedural artifact at a time and aim to produce an equivalent target query. QTRAN targets oracle transfer: it translates a query and regenerates its target-side mutant so that the metamorphic relation is preserved for logic-bug detection. \sedar{} and \tool{} share the goal of transferring existing test cases into fuzzing seeds. For this goal, a transferred seed must execute on the target DBMS, retain testing-relevant SQL structures, and remain parsable and mutable by the downstream fuzzer.
 
-\textbf{Different challenges.} A fuzzing seed is an ordered sequence of independently executed statements, and later statements may depend on the target-side state produced by earlier statements. For example, in \texttt{ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN b INT; SELECT b FROM t;}, the \texttt{SELECT} is valid only if the translated \texttt{ALTER TABLE} has produced the expected column on the target DBMS. When the target dialect changes a type, a constraint, or a DDL form, subsequent statements must follow the translated target state rather than the source state. Translating each statement correctly in isolation is therefore insufficient. Our focus is on maintaining this evolving target-side context across an ordered test sequence, beyond translating a self-contained query or preserving an oracle relation. \sedar{} addresses the same setting but translates each statement using a source-side sub-schema and does not maintain translated target-side context across statements.
+\textbf{Different challenges.} For CrackSQL and RISE, the main challenge is to map the dialect-specific functions, types, and syntax of a single query or procedural artifact to an equivalent target form. For QTRAN, the main challenge is to keep the metamorphic relation between a query and its mutant valid after translation. For \sedar{}, the main challenge is to make transferred test cases executable on the target DBMS and parsable by the downstream fuzzer, which it addresses by translating each statement with its referenced source-side sub-schema and temporarily commenting out incompatible fragments during mutation. For \tool{}, the main challenge is that SQL logic is tightly coupled with dialect-specific syntax, which requires separating syntactic differences while preserving the original structure, cross-statement dependencies, and semantic richness of a test case.
 
 \textbf{Different methods.} AQT is built on a standard SQL AST; its contribution is not the tree representation itself but the cross-statement information it adds and how this information is consumed during transfer. First, AQT records forward read-after-write dependency edges between statements. These edges identify dependency-relevant preceding statements for context selection during instantiation and repair, while the original statement order is retained. Second, AQT records the cumulative schema context of the source test case, including object-state summaries such as object existence, table definitions, column names and types, constraints, and DDL-induced schema changes. During instantiation, \tool{} maintains the corresponding target-side context and updates it after each translated statement. Each statement is thus instantiated and repaired against the translated target state. Third, abstract labels separate dialect-specific constructs from the surrounding structure, so that dialect-aware instantiation remains local while the original structure is preserved. Finally, \tool{} uses parser and execution feedback to repair transferred seeds before mutation, whereas \sedar{} temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them afterward. As described in their papers, the workflows of CrackSQL, RISE, and QTRAN do not propagate target-side context across the statements of a test case. We have updated Table~I and Sections~II (Background and Motivation) and~VI (Related Work) to reflect these differences.
 
